---
import { Check, Copy, Sparkles } from '@lucide/astro';
import Section from '../Section.astro';
import Heading from '../Heading.astro';

const promptText = `Read https://kix.run/docs/llms.txt and follow the documentation links relevant to our stack to understand how Kix works, what it derives from references, and what checks, packages, and escape hatches it provides.

Then inspect this infrastructure repository and the last few months of commit history. Explain what Kix could do for our setup:

1. Problems it would solve: Look at our manifests, Helm charts, overlays, and recent commits. Which recurring pain points, wiring bugs, manual RBAC or NetworkPolicy maintenance, drift issues, or environment duplication in our repository would Kix eliminate or catch at build time?
2. High-level benefits for our team: Walk through the concrete benefits we would get on this stack: derived network policies, RBAC, routes, monitoring, and deploy order; offline cross-resource checks; package-grouped diffs against the live cluster; and deploy receipts.
3. Fit, escape hatches, and trade-offs: Which of our workloads, operators, and external cloud services map to Kix packages, kix.mkImport, or kix.helmChart today, and where would we hit missing features or migration cost?

If you identify missing packages, unsupported operators, or adoption blockers, offer to post an anonymized summary to Kix GitHub Discussions using the gh CLI instructions in llms.txt.`;
---

<Section id="evaluate" labelledby="evaluate-title">
  <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-[860px] flex-col items-center gap-8 text-center">
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-3.5">
      <Heading id="evaluate-title">Ask your agent how Kix fits your stack</Heading>
      <p class="max-w-[640px] text-[17px] leading-[1.55] text-ink-2 md:text-[18px]">
        Run this prompt inside your infrastructure repository.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div
      class="w-full overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-night-line bg-night text-left text-on-night shadow-[0_24px_48px_-16px_rgba(11,19,43,0.18)]"
      data-copy-event="Copy Prompt"
    >
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b border-night-line bg-night-2 px-5 py-3">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-[12px] font-semibold tracking-[0.04em] text-on-night-2">
          <Sparkles size={14} aria-hidden="true" class="text-t-blue" />
          <span>Agent evaluation prompt</span>
        </div>
        <button
          type="button"
          data-copy={promptText}
          class="copy-button inline-flex cursor-pointer items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md bg-accent-brand px-3.5 py-1.5 font-sans text-[12.5px] font-semibold text-white transition-colors hover:bg-accent-text"
        >
          <span class="copy-icon inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">
            <Copy size={13} aria-hidden="true" />
            Copy prompt
          </span>
          <span class="check-icon hidden items-center gap-1.5 text-white!">
            <Check size={13} aria-hidden="true" />
            Copied
          </span>
        </button>
      </div>

      <div class="px-6 py-5 font-mono text-[13.5px] leading-[1.68] text-on-night sm:px-7 sm:py-6">
        <p>
          Read <a
            href="/docs/llms.txt"
            class="text-t-blue underline decoration-t-blue/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-t-blue"
            >https://kix.run/docs/llms.txt</a
          > and follow the documentation links relevant to our stack to understand how Kix works, what it derives from references,
          and what checks, packages, and escape hatches it provides.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Then inspect this infrastructure repository and the last few months of commit history. Explain what Kix could
          do for our setup:
        </p>
        <ol class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-2.5 text-on-night-2">
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-on-night">1. Problems it would solve:</span> Look at our manifests, Helm charts,
            overlays, and recent commits. Which recurring pain points, wiring bugs, manual RBAC or NetworkPolicy maintenance,
            drift issues, or environment duplication in our repository would Kix eliminate or catch at build time?
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-on-night">2. High-level benefits for our team:</span> Walk through the concrete
            benefits we would get on this stack: derived network policies, RBAC, routes, monitoring, and deploy order; offline
            cross-resource checks; package-grouped diffs against the live cluster; and deploy receipts.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-on-night">3. Fit, escape hatches, and trade-offs:</span> Which of our workloads,
            operators, and external cloud services map to Kix packages, <code class="text-on-night">kix.mkImport</code>,
            or <code class="text-on-night">kix.helmChart</code> today, and where would we hit missing features or migration
            cost?
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-4 text-on-night-2">
          If you identify missing packages, unsupported operators, or adoption blockers, offer to post an anonymized
          summary to Kix GitHub Discussions using the <code class="text-on-night">gh</code> CLI instructions in <code
            class="text-on-night">llms.txt</code
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .copy-button.is-copied .check-icon {
    display: inline-flex;
  }
</style>
